  "signature": "(-,+,+,+)",
  "convention": "We use Gaussian electromagnetic units with $c=G=1$.",
  "history": "A current along a cosmic string surrounds it with a magnetic field. Edward Witten showed in 1985 that cosmic strings in certain gauge theories can carry superconducting currents [witten1985superconducting]. Charged bosons can condense in the string core, and charged fermions can occupy modes confined to the string [witten1985superconducting]. Ian Moss and Stephen Poletti found an exterior for a long, straight superconducting string in 1987, including the energy and stress of the magnetic field outside the core [mosspoletti1987superconducting, sen1999superconducting]. Piotr Amsterdamski and Pablo Laguna-Castillo solved the coupled field equations numerically for an infinite bosonic superconducting string in 1988 [amsterdamskilaguna1988internal]. Arif Babul, Tsvi Piran, and David N. Spergel solved the coupled gravitational, electromagnetic, and scalar field equations for an infinite bosonic superconducting string in 1988 [babul1988curvature].¶Marek Demiański calculated test particle and light ray trajectories around a massive conducting string in 1988 [demianski1988conducting]. Bernard Linet treated the weak gravitational field in 1989, and Lech Mankiewicz and Mikołaj Misiak calculated the weak vacuum screening of the string current far from the core that year [linet1989superconducting, mankiewiczmisiak1989vacuum]. Tom Helliwell and Deborah A. Konkowski related three exterior parameters to the current and integrals over the string fields in 1990 [helliwellkonkowski1990linearized]. Linet calculated the conversion of electromagnetic radiation into gravitational radiation around the string in 1990 and found a small conversion efficiency in the WKB approximation [linet1990conversion]. Patrick Peter and Denis Puy matched the exterior to the string core for spacelike and timelike currents in 1993 and calculated light bending to first order in the gravitational constant [peterpuy1993gravitational]. Peter distinguished light deflection from the angular deficit for strings with unequal energy density and tension in 1994 [peter1994comments].¶Restoring $G$ and keeping $c=1$, Konstantinos Dimopoulos wrote a first order exterior in 1998 for a string with energy per unit length $U$, tension $T$, current $J$, and core radius $r_c$ [dimopoulos1998magnetic]. The transverse optical geometry has angular coefficient $1-\\delta/\\pi$, where $\\delta=8\\pi G[U+J^2(1/2+\\ln(r/r_c))]$ at $r>r_c$ in the weak field regime [dimopoulos1998magnetic]. A slowly moving test particle has radial acceleration $-2G[U-T+J^2(1+2\\ln(r/r_c))]/r$ at that order [dimopoulos1998magnetic]. Dimopoulos proposed that the gravitational attraction of moving superconducting strings could generate plasma vorticity and seed primordial magnetic fields [dimopoulos1998magnetic]. Reinaldo J. Gleiser and Manuel H. Tiglio constructed cylindrical solutions of the Einstein-Maxwell equations in 1998 to describe time dependent fields outside a superconducting string [gleisertiglio1998nonstationary]. They recovered the static electrovacuum exterior as a limit in which the soliton width becomes large [gleisertiglio1998nonstationary].¶A regular source satisfying the strong energy condition has a lower bound on its radius in the parameter range where the ideal line singularity repels test particles [gleisertiglio1998nonstationary]. Time dependent exterior fields alone do not determine a unique current inside that source [gleisertiglio1998nonstationary]. Anjan Ananda Sen obtained two families of far exterior geometries in Brans-Dicke theory in 1999 [sen1999superconducting]. One family reduces to the Moss-Poletti exterior when the scalar field becomes constant [sen1999superconducting]. Cristine Nunes Ferreira and coauthors matched a general scalar-tensor exterior to the string core in the weak field approximation in 2000 [ferreira2000current]. The exterior has five parameters related to the string structure and to a massless scalar field that varies logarithmically with radius [ferreira2000current].¶Ernesto Fabian Eiroa and Claudio Simeone joined two copies of the exterior at a cylindrical shell to construct wormholes in 2004 [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. The exact far exterior can be written with $x=r/\\ell$ and $F=(x^m+kx^{-m})/(1+k)$, where $\\ell$ is a scaling length, $k\\geq 0$, and $m$ is a constant [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. In Gaussian electromagnetic units with $c=G=1$, its line element is $ds^2=F^2[x^{2m^2}(-dt^2+dr^2)+W^2r^2d\\phi^2]+F^{-2}dz^2$, with angular scale $W>0$ [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. The circumference around the string is $2\\pi WrF$, and the current is $I=\\pm mW\\sqrt{k}/(1+k)$ [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. The exterior electromagnetic tensor has magnetic component $F_{zr}=\\pm 2m(1+k)\\sqrt{k}x^{2m}/[r(x^{2m}+k)^2]$ [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. When $k=0$, the current and magnetic field vanish and the exterior becomes a static vacuum cylinder [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical].¶The shell has negative surface energy density [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. Keeping the static relations between the surface tensions and energy density during motion, Eiroa and Simeone found that a perturbed throat expands or contracts according to the sign of its initial radial velocity [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. Setting $m=0$ leaves a conical exterior with circumference $2\\pi Wr$ [eiroasimeone2004cylindrical]. Eiroa and Simeone extended the construction to general static cylindrical geometries in 2010 [eiroasimeone2010aspects]. Under the same assumption about the surface equations of state, the throat moves monotonically after a velocity perturbation and cannot oscillate about an equilibrium radius [eiroasimeone2010aspects]. A throat defined by a minimum circumference can have positive surface energy density in a radial electric field; a throat carrying an axial current has negative surface energy density [eiroasimeone2010aspects].",
